From: Project Luma
Subject: [PATCH] luma: one ink and shadow rule for every surface over the wallpaper

Glass painted white ink on a 31%-white veil, so over a light wallpaper the
Shelf islands, the clock, the Well and the beacon measured 1.00:1 -- text the
same colour as the surface under it. Frost was authored as "light with a blur"
and rode the opaque light rules, so it carried dark ink; its secondary line
measured 3.21:1. Nothing in the Shell chrome had ever had a text or icon
shadow: the only text-shadow in the tree was the login screen's. Across the
Shelf islands, the Well, the clock, Quick Options, notifications, the beacon,
the sharing indicator and the administrator prompt, 32 text-and-icon pairings
were below WCAG AA.

Each of those surfaces also kept its own opinion about ink: a CSS block per
treatment in luma-shelf.css, a STATUS_INK table in panel.js, a
$note-shelf-ink map in the notification Sass, and again in the seal and share
sheets. They agreed by hand and drifted by hand.

So there is now one ledger, SURFACE_INK in lumaSurfaceMaterials.js, next to
the blur recipes it belongs with. It names, per appearance mode, the veil at
three depths, primary and secondary ink, one accent, one hairline and one
shadow. panel.js reads it instead of its own table; the stylesheets carry its
values; and one rule at the end of luma-shelf.css puts colour and text-shadow
on the surface root, which St inherits to every label beneath, plus one
descendant rule for glyph shadows, because icon-shadow does not inherit.

The owner asked for white ink in light, frost and glass. Measured over Luma's
four shipped wallpapers plus plain white and plain black, at 100% and 200%:

  - White ink on opaque paper is 1.12:1, and 1.64:1 under a fully opaque 6px
    shadow. White ink on the milky frost was 1.08:1, and 1.59:1 under the same
    shadow. A light veil cannot carry white ink at any shadow strength, so
    paper keeps dark ink and says so in the test.
  - Frost and glass instead become smoked veils, rgba(28,33,40,0.86) and
    rgba(14,19,26,0.78), which is what makes white ink legitimate. Every frost
    rule that was grouped with light moves to the smoked values with it, so no
    surface is left on a light fill behind white ink. The veils are deeper
    than resting legibility needs: a hover or pressed fill stacks on top and a
    lift on a translucent veil spends white-ink contrast, so the budget has to
    hold for veil + recess + pressed. Sized only for rest, glass dropped to
    3.08:1 the moment a recessed target was pressed. What separates frost from
    glass is the blur radius and saturation, not opacity alone.
  - The shadow is 0 1px 3px rgba(6,9,13,0.80) on the smoked modes and none on
    the opaque ones, where there is no wallpaper behind the surface and a
    shadow only smears the ink. St carries one shadow per property, so this is
    one shadow, not a stack. It stays crisp at 100% and 125%.
  - Secondary ink was below AA in all four modes at 11px (3.61:1 on paper,
    3.21:1 on frost, 1.00:1 on glass) and is raised in all four, with dark's
    sized for the pressed notification card rather than the resting one.
  - Screen sharing and recording pinned GNOME's amber on the whole indicator,
    which failed on paper at 1.46:1 and on both translucent modes. The label
    is now the surface's ink and the amber stays on the glyph, which has only
    the 3:1 threshold to clear: a darker amber on paper, and a brighter one on
    the smoked veils, because the Well's recess lifts them by 5% white and
    GNOME's own amber measures 2.86:1 there on glass.

That takes the 32 failures to 0. The worst case left is 5.57:1 for the
smallest secondary text at rest and 4.68:1 for it under a recess plus a
pressed fill, against a 4.5 threshold, with the shadow discounted entirely.

Two things the first cut of this got wrong, both found by measuring rather
than reading, and both fixed here:

  - Fifteen rules gave an opaque treatment a smoked value. light and dark were
    authored grouped with frost and glass, so re-tinting a shared selector list
    moved all of it. The worst of those put white ink on the opaque light
    status cluster -- 1.12:1, the exact failure this patch exists to prevent --
    and dissolved the light plate under the Stop pill, leaving its near-black
    label on the veil. The opaque selectors are now split out with the values
    they had.
  - The ledger's own test could not see any of that, because it gates the
    ledger and not the stylesheets that spend it. data/theme/verify-treatment-ink.py
    closes that gap from %build: an opaque light surface may not carry
    near-white ink, and a smoked surface may not carry near-black ink unless
    the rule lays down a light plate for it to sit on. Run against the first
    cut it reports all seven remaining faults; against this one it passes.

Found while handing the sharing picker's ink to its own owner: the same
dark-label-on-hover shape existed in this patch's own Seal card.
.luma-seal-allow:hover inherited a near-black fill on the smoked treatments
with no frost/glass override, so the Allow button's ink disappeared on
:hover. Fixed the same way as its resting state.

data/theme/verify-treatment-ink.py gains a second pass for exactly that
shape: a class that paints dark ink at rest, and an unqualified :hover,
:checked or :active rule for that same class with a near-black fill and no
frost/glass override, so the ink and the fill go dark together. This is a
cross-rule check the first pass cannot make, because the light plate and the
dark state fill are two different rules and neither is wrong by itself.

Capture (the screenshot and recording tool) carries the same three-treatment
card the Seal and Share sheets started from: frost was still the milky light
card, only glass was smoked. Re-tinted the same way, deepened to the same
0.88/0.82 the state-fill fix settled on so it has the same pressed-state
headroom, and its Go/Stop button's own two-tone plate (dark-filled on frost,
light-filled on glass -- a pre-existing choice, not an ink-direction bug) is
left as is. Its one incidental find: the timer sub-label under the Go button
had the same dark ink on both smoked treatments, invisible on frost's dark
plate; split to match each plate's own ink.

The notification Sass carried its own hover and pressed values, set before
the ledger's state fix and never brought back in line: the beacon still
pressed at 0.22 on frost and glass, one of the stacks measured failing, and
dark's beacon ink2 was still 0.66. Both the beacon map and the cards' own
states now carry the ledger's hover and press, so the gate that measures
veilCard under those states is measuring what actually ships.

The guard itself had two blind spots, both found by building the series
from patches alone rather than from a working tree. It checked a hand-kept
list of sheets, which never included luma-capture.css after this patch began
re-tinting it -- so Capture's Go button and Stop pill shipped with the same
dissolved-plate bug fixed in Seal and Share, and frost's Go hover put light
ink on a light fill. It now finds every sheet that styles a smoked treatment.
And it only honoured a plate in one direction; white ink on a dark plate the
rule paints itself (a primary button on paper) is now exempt the same way,
inactive states are exempt as WCAG 1.4.3 exempts them, and ink that sits on
a parent's plate is waived only by a named comment above the rule.
Capture's Go and Stop become one filled control on both smoked cards -- a
solid light plate with dark ink, as Share's and Seal's are.

tests/unit/lumaSurfaceInk.js gates the ledger: every veil depth against every
ink role, in every state a target can present -- resting, hovered, pressed,
and each of those inside the Well's recess -- over pure white and pure black,
the two extremes any wallpaper lies between. It also pins the three decisions
that are easy to undo by accident: that the opaque modes take no shadow, that
paper must not take white ink, and that a lifting state fill stays inside the
veil's budget.

Window title bars are not in here. Luma draws them from the app kit, whose
frost and glass window surfaces are still near-opaque light, where white ink
measures 1.04:1 and 1.09:1. Those veils move with this one, on the app kit's
own release.

The sharing picker's card follows the same ledger, but its stylesheet is
created by 0149, so that ink travels with 0149 rather than here. That keeps
this patch free to build ahead of the picker while the picker is held.

Generated against the Shell .107 tree (0149 applied) for release .108.
